Here's a straightforward take on quicksort—clean, efficient, and handles most cases well without overcomplicating things:

```
function quicksort(array, low, high):
    if low < high:
        pivot_index = partition(array, low, high)
        quicksort(array, low, pivot_index - 1)
        quicksort(array, pivot_index + 1, high)

function partition(array, low, high):
    pivot = array[high]
    i = low - 1

    for j from low to high - 1:
        if array[j] <= pivot:
            i = i + 1
            swap array[i] with array[j]

    swap array[i + 1] with array[high]
    return i + 1

// initial call
quicksort(my_array, 0, length(my_array) - 1)
```

If you want something even simpler to implement (though slower for large datasets), bubble sort asks very little of you:

```
function bubble_sort(array):
    n = length(array)

    for i from 0 to n - 1:
        for j from 0 to n - i - 2:
            if array[j] > array[j + 1]:
                swap array[j] with array[j + 1]
```

Both get the job done—just depends on whether you're optimizing for clarity or performance.
